function _sfc_render(_ctx, _cache, $props, $setup, $data, $options) {
  return openBlock(), createElementBlock("div", null, [..._cache[0] || (_cache[0] = [
    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="_3-16-ai-model-comprehensive-practice-field" tabindex="-1">3.16 AI Model Comprehensive Practice Field <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-16-ai-model-comprehensive-practice-field" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.16 AI Model Comprehensive Practice Field&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="_3-16-1-lesson-introduction" tabindex="-1">3.16.1 Lesson Introduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-16-1-lesson-introduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.16.1 Lesson Introduction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This lesson serves as the culminating finale of the course series, requiring the comprehensive application of all sensors, actuators, and three large models encompassing language, voice, and vision learned in the previous three lessons to independently design and implement a fully functional intelligent robot project. The course provides two reference options, namely a light-sensing robot and a pipe inspection robot, while also encouraging free creativity. Through 15 minutes of independent development, the project encompasses the complete workflow: requirements definition, hardware assembly, programming and debugging, and presentation and optimization. This process thoroughly consolidates all acquired knowledge.</p><h2 id="_3-16-2-learning-objectives" tabindex="-1">3.16.2 Learning Objectives <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-16-2-learning-objectives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.16.2 Learning Objectives&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p><strong>Knowledge Objectives</strong>: Consolidate the core functions of large language, voice, and vision models, and understand the basic architecture of multimodal integration.</p></li><li><p><strong>Skill Objectives</strong>: Independently plan projects, assemble hardware, write comprehensive programs, and achieve multi-sensor linkage and model collaboration.</p></li><li><p><strong>Thinking Objectives</strong>: Cultivate &quot;system design&quot; thinking by starting from functional requirements, selecting appropriate modules, and rationally allocating resources.</p></li><li><p><strong>Application Objectives</strong>: Stimulate innovation awareness and migrate learned technologies to solving real-world problems.</p></li></ol><h2 id="_3-16-3-preparation" tabindex="-1">3.16.3 Preparation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-16-3-preparation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.16.3 Preparation&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-hardware-preparation" tabindex="-1">(1) Hardware Preparation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-hardware-preparation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(1) Hardware Preparation&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Controller: AiBlock controller</li><li>AI core module: WonderMind large model module</li><li>Input sensors may be selected and combined as needed, including ultrasonic sensor, light sensor, and temperature and humidity sensor</li><li>Motion modules: 360° block motor, 180° block servo</li><li>Audio-visual output modules: RGB light module, dot matrix module</li><li>Others: 4-pin module cable, 3-pin servo cable, Type-C data cable, building block parts</li></ul><h3 id="_2-software-preparation" tabindex="-1">(2) Software Preparation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-software-preparation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(2) Software Preparation&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>WonderLab programming software</li></ul><h3 id="_3-pre-lesson-preview-tasks" tabindex="-1">(3) Pre-Lesson Preview Tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-pre-lesson-preview-tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(3) Pre-Lesson Preview Tasks&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Independently review core knowledge points from previous lessons:</p><p>① What are the three WonderMind models and their respective functions.</p><p>② Wiring interface specifications for various sensors across sensor ports <strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong>, <strong>C</strong>, and <strong>D</strong> supporting I2C ports.</p><p>③ Motor and servo corresponding <strong>S1</strong> to <strong>S4</strong> power interface distinctions.</p><p>④ Standard device power-on / off, network pairing, and MCP configuration operation sequence.</p><h2 id="_3-16-4-hands-on-project" tabindex="-1">3.16.4 Hands-On Project <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-16-4-hands-on-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.16.4 Hands-On Project&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-pre-lesson-knowledge-review" tabindex="-1">(1) Pre-Lesson Knowledge Review <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-pre-lesson-knowledge-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(1) Pre-Lesson Knowledge Review&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li>First, independently consider the following questions to complete knowledge consolidation:</li></ol><p>The vision large model = AI&#39;s eyes, capable of face, object, color, and text recognition.</p><p>The voice large model = AI ears + mouth, responsible for voice recognition, voice broadcast, and voice dialogue.</p><p>The large language model = AI brain, parsing text and voice instructions to make logical judgments.</p><p>A single AI module has limited functionality. Only the combination of multiple models + sensors + motors can complete complex intelligent tasks.</p><ol start="2"><li>Independently categorize all kit modules into three hardware categories:</li></ol><p>Controller: AiBlock Main Controller, the central command hub for all devices.</p><p>Input perception modules, which collect external information: WonderMind module, ultrasonic sensor, light sensor, and temperature and humidity sensor.</p><p>The output and power module, which receives commands and performs actions, includes the RGB light, dot matrix display, 360-degree building block motor, and 180-degree building block servo.</p><ol start="3"><li>Independent thinking task: If building an inspection robot, which hardware is needed? What role does each component play?</li></ol><h3 id="_2-reference-case-independent-development-guide" tabindex="-1">(2) Reference Case Independent Development Guide <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-reference-case-independent-development-guide" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(2) Reference Case Independent Development Guide&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><h4 id="case-1-light-sensing-robot" tabindex="-1">Case 1: Light-Sensing Robot <a class="header-anchor" href="#case-1-light-sensing-robot" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Case 1: Light-Sensing Robot&quot;">​</a></h4></li></ul><ol><li>Project functional requirements:</li></ol><p>The robot autonomously advances at low speed in dark environments. The light-sensitive sensor continuously detects ambient light intensity, and when the light suddenly brightens due to factors such as approaching a window or light source obstruction, combined with the ultrasonic sensor detecting nearby obstacles, both conditions are simultaneously satisfied to trigger the RGB red light flashing as a warning, the dot matrix display showing alert characters, and the WonderMind voice broadcasting &quot;Obstacle ahead, please detour.&quot; After voice wake-up, voice commands can control the robot to stop or continue moving forward.</p><ol start="2"><li>Independent hardware selection and wiring guide:</li></ol><ul><li>Hardware inventory for self-checking and retrieval</li></ul><p>AiBlock controller, WonderMind module, light sensor, ultrasonic sensor, 360° block motor, RGB light module</p><ul><li>Standard wiring to be completed independently</li></ul><p>Connect the WonderMind module to port <strong>A</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</p><p>Connect the light sensor to port <strong>B</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</p><p>Connect the ultrasonic sensor to port <strong>C</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</p><p>Connect the left motor to port <strong>S1</strong> of the controller using a 3-pin servo cable.</p><p>Connect the right motor to port <strong>S2</strong> of the controller using a 3-pin servo cable.</p><p>Connect the RGB light module to port <strong>D</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</p><ol start="3"><li>Independent assembly steps:</li></ol><p>Use standard blocks to build a triangular tracked chassis and fix the left and right motors. Reserve space at the front of the chassis for the ultrasonic sensor. Fix the WonderMind module on the top bracket, install the light sensor on the side, and secure the RGB light module with side clips to complete the assembly.</p><ol start="4"><li>Independent WonderLab programming software steps:</li></ol><ul><li>Open the software and create a blank project.</li><li>Add extensions: select <strong>AI Blocks Board</strong>, <strong>WonderMind</strong>, <strong>360 Motor</strong>, <strong>Light sensor</strong>, <strong>Ultrasonic</strong>, and <strong>RGB module</strong>.</li><li>Independently build initialization blocks:</li></ul><p>Power-on initialization of WonderMind, loading MCP configuration, activating dialogue channel.</p><p>Initialization of light and ultrasonic sensors.</p><p>Power-on buzzer playing startup sound effect.</p><ul><li>Independently design main loop logic:</li></ul><p>① Continuously read light value and ultrasonic distance.</p><p>② Judgment condition: light &gt; 70 and distance &lt; 15 cm.</p><p>③ Warning condition satisfied: RGB red light flashes, dot matrix displays WARN, WonderMind voice broadcasts warning.</p><p>④ Condition not satisfied: RGB turns off, motors run straight at low speed.</p><ul><li>Voice interaction branch: read the returned dialogue text from WonderMind, recognize &quot;forward&quot; and &quot;stop&quot; voice commands, and control motor startup and stopping.</li><li>Program self-check: verify completeness of initialization, loop, and conditional judgment blocks.</li></ul><ol start="5"><li>Device downloading and debugging to be performed independently:</li></ol><ul><li>Strictly follow power-on sequence: first turn on controller power, then turn on WonderMind module.</li><li>Wait for WonderMind module to complete WiFi network pairing and MCP configuration interface.</li><li>Connect computer and controller via Type-C, select corresponding port to upload program.</li><li>After upload completes, restart WonderMind, press the left button on the controller to start the program.</li><li>Independently troubleshoot:</li></ul><p>① Sensor no data: check whether A / B / C interface wiring is firmly inserted.</p><p>② No voice broadcast: confirm MCP configuration block has been added.</p><p>③ Motor not moving: verify <strong>S1</strong> / <strong>S2</strong> interface matches program motor numbering.</p><ul><li><h4 id="case-2-pipeline-inspection-robot" tabindex="-1">Case 2: Pipeline Inspection Robot <a class="header-anchor" href="#case-2-pipeline-inspection-robot" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Case 2: Pipeline Inspection Robot&quot;">​</a></h4></li></ul><ol><li>Project functional requirements:</li></ol><p>Simulate narrow pipeline internal inspection scenario. The tracked robot continues straight ahead. The ultrasonic sensor detects front obstacle distance in real time. The dot matrix dynamically displays centimeter values in real time. When distance is less than 10 cm, the robot immediately stops, the RGB yellow light stays on, and WonderMind voice broadcasts &quot;Obstacle detected ahead.&quot; Voice commands &quot;turn left / turn right&quot; are issued, and the robot executes turning actions.</p><ol start="2"><li>Independent hardware selection and wiring guide:</li></ol><ul><li>Hardware inventory for independent reference and retrieval</li></ul><p>AiBlock controller, WonderMind module, ultrasonic sensor, 360° block motor, RGB light module, dot matrix module</p><ul><li>standard wiring independently complete</li></ul><p>Connect the WonderMind module to port <strong>A</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</p><p>Connect the ultrasonic sensor to port <strong>B</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</p><p>Connect the left motor to port <strong>S1</strong> of the controller using a 3-pin servo cable.</p><p>Connect the right motor to port <strong>S2</strong> of the controller using a 3-pin servo cable.</p><p>Connect the dot matrix module to port <strong>C</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</p><p>Connect the RGB light module to port <strong>D</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</p><ol start="3"><li>Independent assembly steps:</li></ol><p>Build a narrow tracked chassis adapted to confined pipeline space. Fix the ultrasonic sensor at the front facing the forward direction. Install the dot matrix display at the middle of the body for convenient distance value observation. Fix the WonderMind module on the top bracket.</p><ol start="4"><li>Independent WonderLab programming software steps:</li></ol><ul><li>Open the software and create a blank project.</li><li>Under the extensions menu, independently select and add: AI blocks board, WonderMind large model, 360° motor, light sensor, ultrasonic sensor, and RGB module.</li><li>Independently build initialization blocks:</li></ul><p>Power-on initialization of all modules, completing large model MCP basic configuration.</p><ul><li>Independently design main loop logic:</li></ul><p>① Loop reading ultrasonic distance, dot matrix real-time printing of numbers.</p><p>② Distance &lt; 10 cm: all motors stop, RGB yellow light turns on, voice alarm.</p><p>③ Distance &gt; 10 cm: motors run straight at uniform speed, RGB turns off.</p><ul><li>Voice interaction branch: recognize left turn / right turn text, execute differential steering logic.</li><li>After program completion, independently sort out the signal flow: ultrasonic data acquisition → main controller judgment → motor / light / voice execution feedback.</li></ul><ol start="5"><li>Autonomous device download and debugging:</li></ol><ul><li>Strictly follow power-on sequence: first turn on controller power, then turn on WonderMind module.</li><li>Wait for WonderMind module to complete WiFi network pairing and MCP configuration interface.</li><li>Connect computer and controller via Type-C, select corresponding port to upload program.</li><li>After upload completes, restart WonderMind, press the left button on the controller to start the program.</li><li>Independent testing: move hand close to ultrasonic sensor, observe whether dot matrix value changes, robot stops, and voice prompt trigger synchronously.</li></ul><h2 id="_3-16-5-free-creative-expansion-with-independent-innovation-tasks" tabindex="-1">3.16.5 Free Creative Expansion with Independent Innovation Tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-16-5-free-creative-expansion-with-independent-innovation-tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.16.5 Free Creative Expansion with Independent Innovation Tasks&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Not limited to the two reference cases, students may combine life scenarios to independently design brand-new AI robots. Creative reference directions provided:</p><p>① Smart Environment Care Robot: Temperature and humidity + light sensors, voice broadcast indoor temperature and humidity, simulate watering action when humidity is low.</p><p>② Face Greeting Robot: Vision recognizes face, after recognition light changes color, voice greets, chassis rotates.</p><p>③ Item Sorting Robot: Vision recognizes red / blue colors, corresponding left / right turn sorting.</p><h2 id="_3-16-6-analysis-and-summary-through-independent-review-and-consolidation" tabindex="-1">3.16.6 Analysis and Summary through Independent Review and Consolidation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-16-6-analysis-and-summary-through-independent-review-and-consolidation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.16.6 Analysis and Summary through Independent Review and Consolidation&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-core-knowledge-independent-review" tabindex="-1">(1) Core Knowledge Independent Review <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-core-knowledge-independent-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(1) Core Knowledge Independent Review&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li>Three AI model division of labor:</li></ol><ul><li>The voice large model is responsible for auditory recognition and voice broadcasting, functioning as the human ears and mouth.</li><li>The vision large model is responsible for image acquisition and object, face, and text recognition, serving as the human eyes.</li><li>The large language model is responsible for semantic understanding and instruction parsing, functioning as the human brain for thinking.</li></ul><ol start="2"><li><p>Core linkage carrier: AiBlock controller serves as the center, receiving data from AI modules and sensors, and issuing instructions to control motors, lights, and other execution components.</p></li><li><p>Complete intelligent closed loop: sensors / vision / voice collect external information → controller + large model logical judgment → motors, audio-visual modules execute feedback.</p></li></ol><h3 id="_2-life-skills-transfer-through-independent-thinking" tabindex="-1">(2) Life Skills Transfer through Independent Thinking <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-life-skills-transfer-through-independent-thinking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(2) Life Skills Transfer through Independent Thinking&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Classroom multimodal fusion technology is widely applied in commercial smart devices on the market:</p><p>Household sweeping robots that integrate visual obstacle avoidance, voice control, and distance sensors, restaurant delivery robots, park inspection vehicles, and intelligent greenhouse maintenance equipment all employ the same &quot;perception-decision-execution&quot; technical architecture at the underlying level.</p><h2 id="_3-16-7-technical-support-and-product-discussion" tabindex="-1">3.16.7 Technical Support and Product Discussion <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-16-7-technical-support-and-product-discussion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.16.7 Technical Support and Product Discussion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If creative ideas emerge after using the product, sharing in the <a href="https://forum.hiwonder.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hiwonder community</a> is welcome:</p><a href="https://forum.hiwonder.com.cn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img loading="lazy" height="640" width="1136" src="' + _imports_12 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"></a>', 101)
  ])]);
}
